avior\n[L1045] manual override\n\n[L1047] Exact values remain open.\n\n[L1049] ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n[L1050] 9. WHY AUDIO IS THE RIGHT FIRST TEST\n[L1051] ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n[L1053] Audio ducking tests several architectural requirements simultaneously:\n\n[L1055] - continuous values;\n[L1056] - state capture;\n[L1057] - state restoration;\n[L1058] - deterministic execution;\n[L1059] - physical trigger;\n[L1060] - VoiceBridge trigger;\n[L1061] - easy visual/auditory verification;\n[L1062] - low authority risk;\n[L1063] - immediate utility.\n\n[L1065] If audio duck/restore works through the normalized event contract, the control\n[L1066] plane is probably healthy enough to expand.\n\n[L1068] ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n[L1069] 10. NORMALIZED CONTROL EVENT CONTRACT\n[L1070] ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n[L1072] The durable architecture is event-first, not device-first.\n\n[L1074] Working conceptual event:\n\n{\n[L1077]   \"source\": \"midi|gesture|voice|touch|keyboard\",\n[L1078]   \"device_id\": \"optional\",\n[L1079]   \"control\": \"fader|rotate|tap|cue|swipe|hold|pinch\",\n[L1080]   \"value\": 0.42,\n[L1081]   \"direction\": \"up|down|left|right|clockwise|counterclockwise\",\n[L1082]   \"velocity\": 0.31,\n[L1083]   \"duration_ms\": 480,\n[L1084]   \"confidence\": 0.96,\n[L1085]   \"context\": \"music|seat|handoff|surface|global\",\n[L1086]   \"timestamp\": \"...\",\n[L1087]   \"correlation_id\": \"...\"\n}\n\n[L1090] Not every event needs every field.\n\n[L1092] The contract should remain small.\n\n[L1094] Do not encode hardware-specific semantics downstream if avoidable.\n\n[L1096] ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n[L1097] 11. COMMAND LAYER\n[L1098] ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n[L1100] Normalized events may resolve into bounded commands such as:\n\n[L1102] AUDIO_DUCK\n[L1103] AUDIO_RESTORE\n[L1104] AUDIO_SET\n[L1105] FOCUS_SEAT\n[L1106] CUE_SEAT\n[L1107] NEXT_HANDOFF\n[L1108] PREVIOUS_HANDOFF\n[L1109] ROUTE_HANDOFF\n[L1110] HOLD_HANDOFF\n[L1111] ACK_HANDOFF\n[L1112] FOCUS_MAIN_DEVPAD\n[L1113] FOCUS_ROOKIE_DEVPAD\n\n[L1115] The distinction matters:\n\n[L1117] gesture event\n!=\n[L1119] command\n\n[L1121] Example:\n\n[L1123] clockwise rotation\n\n[L1125] Context = music\n[L1126] → AUDIO_UP\n\n[L1128] Context = seat selector\n[L1129] → NEXT_SEAT\n\n[L1131] Context = handoff queue\n[L1132] → NEXT_HANDOFF\n\n[L1134] Same physical gesture.\n[L1135] Different bounded interpretation.\n\n[L1137] ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n[L1138] 12. GESTURE CONTROL\n[L1139] ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n[L1141] Don explicitly wants to explore hand/finger operation such as:\n\n[L1143] - finger up;\n[L1144] - finger down;\n[L1145] - left/right;\n[L1146] - circular movement;\n[L1147] - cue;\n[L1148] - point;\n[L1149] - pinch;\n[L1150] - hold;\n[L1151] - flick;\n[L1152] - potentially hand position.\n\n[L1154] Potential conceptual mappings:\n\n[L1156] finger up/down\n[L1157] → increase/decrease selected value\n\n[L1159] left/right\n[L1160] → move between channels/seats\n\n[L1162] clockwise circle\n[L1163] → increase / advance\n\n[L1165] counterclockwise circle\n[L1166] → decrease / back\n\n[L1168] point/cue\n[L1169] → focus or arm selected target\n\npinch\n[L1172] → select\n\nhold\n[L1175] → park/hold\n\nflick\n[L1178] → next/previous\n\n[L1180] These are examples only.\n\n[L1182] No gesture vocabulary is ratified.\n\n[L1184] ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n[L1185] 13. REFERENCE HARDWARE\n[L1186] ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\n[L1188] 