otal the individual file sizes.\n\nSecond, this Drive has nested estates. My first attempt to recursively crawl every top-level folder in one shot hit the connector's tool-call ceiling. So I can audit it, but it needs to be done **folder-by-folder rather than one giant recursive sweep**.\n\nThat means the current evidence says something useful already:\n\n> **We have not found the storage hog yet.**\n\nIt is almost certainly **not Google Docs or Sheets**. We need to hunt for the things that actually consume meaningful space:\n\n- videos\n- MP3/audio\n- PDFs\n- ZIPs/backups\n- raw images\n- old uploaded Office files\n- datasets\n- binary exports\n- potentially large material buried several levels deep\n\nAlso, shared folders such as **Simple Balance** and **Simple balance shared mp3s** need special treatment: seeing them in your Drive does **not by itself prove their contents count against your quota**. Ownership matters.\n\n### Where I would hit next\n\nThe high-value storage recon order is:\n\n1. **CasaCompanion** — finish the nested shared `CasaCompanion` branch; `videos` itself is currently empty.\n2. **Simple Balance**\n3. **Simple balance shared mp3s**\n4. **Tasker → data**\n5. **MRP**\n6. **Colab Notebooks**\n7. **GCE itself**\n8. Then search the entire estate specifically for **large binary/media files**.\n\nAnd one key distinction: cleaning `TEMP`, `IGNORE_TEMP_DELETE_LATER`, etc. is still worth doing for hygiene, but **it will not solve the storage problem**.\n\nThe Cable Car handoff explicitly calls for this recon before cleanup, so we're still on the intended path. fileciteturn0file0L681-L693\n\n**Current storage verdict: we need the deep size pass, not the cosmetic cleanup pass.**", "text_chars": 3016, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "ea63e358-b3ab-4243-805f-3b435715f83e", "role":