 A bare npm run run polls an empty in-memory fake forever with no error. The one thing that made the inbound proving runs real was an environment variable that no file, script, or log in this project records.\n4. The loop scaffolding is thinner than expected, but the documentation is fictional. Only one gate doc exists against 37 technical artifacts — Slices 3 and 4 are labels on real capabilities with no code identity, Slices 5 and 6 do not exist, and the stated Coda/Don review gate was skipped 18 minutes before Slice 1 landed. The real contamination is that three contracts and one recon doc describe a system that does not match the code, and a proven contract violation was accepted and logged as success.\nAnd one thing nobody has accounted for: the production SQLite ledger contains an event type that exists in no source file in this repository, written by a mechanism that is not in the code. Until that is explained, the ledger's own integrity as a record of real operations is not established.\nGCE RECON FOOTER\nMODE:\nREAD-ONLY\n\nFILES MODIFIED:\nNONE\n\nDRIVE MODIFIED:\nNONE  (no Drive API call issued; no Drive read path exists on this host)\n\nDB MODIFIED:\nNONE  (production cable_car.sqlite SHA-256 identical before/after: 4358A0A0…01B17;\n       never opened for write; all SQL ran against a temp copy)\n\nSUB-AGENTS USED:\n[1] LOCAL CODE / IMPLEMENTATION TRUTH\n[2] TEST / REGRESSION TRUTH\n[3] SQLITE / STATE / EVENT TRUTH\n[4] GOOGLE DRIVE / TRANSPORT TRUTH\n[5] AUTH / CONFIG / CAPABILITY TRUTH\n[6] HANDOFF / OPERATIONAL TRUTH\n[7] LOOP / PROCESS CONTAMINATION\n\nCONTRADICTIONS:\n26 material  (3 further sub-agent conflicts, resolved by stated precedence)\n\nUNKNOWN ITEMS:\n20 discrete  (Drive estate fully unobservable; 9 of the 20 are Drive-side)\n\nCURRENT TRUTH CONFIDENCE:\n87%  — high on everything local (code read line-by-line, ledger dumped in full,\n      hashes verified byte-for-byte). The Drive leg is unverified and could\n      move rows in either direction; it is carved out of that number, not\n      averaged into it.\n\nBUILD CONTINUED:\nNO\n\nCERTIFICATION ISSUED:\nNO\nSTATUS:            CHECKPOINT\nCONFIDENCE:        87%\nOUTCOME:           7-lane forensic recon complete. Inbound leg established as\n                   real and live-proven. Outbound leg established as never\n                   built. Loop scaffolding established as thinner than claimed\n                   but the documentation as materially fictional.\nARTIFACTS UPDATED: NONE — read-only recon, no durable artifact written.\n                   This report exists only in session output. Don's call\n                   whether it becomes a handoff.\nDECISIONS:         None made. No finding was acted on. Nothing fixed.\nOPEN ITEMS:        (1) Who generated `RECOVERY_LIVE_FAULT_INJECTED` in the\n                   production ledger — unresolved, and it undermines ledger\n                   integrity as a record of real operations. (2) The\n                   unreproducible real-mode activation. (3) The entire Drive\n                   estate, unobservable from this host. (4) Whether the 89\n                   tests pass — suite not executed under read-only constraint.\nEXPERTISE NEEDED:  YES — Nico (the inert `.env` mode flag and the unreachable\n                   `RECOVERY_LIVE_FAULT_INJECTED` are implementation-bound);\n                   Coda (the three unenforced contracts and the skipped\n                   review gate are governance-bound).\nNEXT ROUTE:        Don + Coda\nANCHOR STATUS:     Advanceable. The question Don posed — \"what do we actually\n                   have, what works, what's missing, what was scaffolding\" —\n                   is answered with evidence on every row except the Drive\n                   estate, which is UNKNOWN and stays UNKNOWN.\nDON INPUT NEEDED:  Yes — (a) whether to fund the outbound leg at all, which is\n                   the only thing standing between this and a round trip;\n                   (b) who can explain the production fault-injection event;\n                   (c) whether Drive-side truth can be obtained, since no read\n                   path exists here and no agent can close that gap read-only.\nHANDOFF: NONE — no downstream artifact was authorized. This report is session output only. If Don wants it durable, the receiving-seat path is GCE_ORG\\02_COUNCILS\\01_Executive_Council\\Operations\\06_HANDOFFS\\Don\\ (Don + Coda are the actual next decision recipients, so SHARED would be wrong).\nNEXT ROUTE: Don + Coda", "text_chars": 5583, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["contracts/return.json"], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "961515d9-3155-4553-b08b-153783c16ed7", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365844.706761, "updated_at": 1790365844.706761, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "model_editable_context", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "ec476471-fecc-4857-ae80-1d47af952b9a", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365850.29502, "updated_at": 1790365850.29502, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "reasoning_recap", "part_kinds": [], "text": "", "text_chars": 0, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "8a09d620-385f-4e6d-82ff-4a39b9ac5fc9", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365850.298333, "updated_at": 1790365894.310742, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes. **This is the reset point.** Space Bunny/Luce found something materially different from the state we had been carrying, and we should treat this recon as the new **local-evidence baseline**, with Drive explicitly carved out as UNKNOWN.\n\n# Coda review — Cable Car recon\n\n## Status: CHECKPOINT\n**Confidence:** high on local state, bounded by Luce’s stated 87%  \n**Mode:** review only  \n**No build action authorized**\n\nThe biggest correction is that we were carrying a much more mature system in our heads than the repository actually contains.\n\nThe locally supported truth is:\n\n```text\nINBOUND:      REAL / LIVE-PROVEN\nOUTBOUND:     NOT BUILT\nROUND TRIP:   NEVER EXISTED\nRUNTIME MODE: NOT REPRODUCIBLE FROM REPO STATE\nCONTRACTS:    PRESENT BUT NOT ENFORCED\nDOCS:         MATERIALLY AHEAD OF IMPLEMENTATION\nDRIVE STATE:  UNKNOWN IN THIS RECON\n```\n\nThat is the frame I would use now.\n\n## The four findings that matter most\n\n### 1. Inbound is legitimate\n\nThis is the strongest positive finding.\n\nLuce did not conclude “everything was fake.” Quite the opposite: the inbound leg appears to be real engineering:\n\n- SHA-256 verification;\n- atomic local delivery;\n- real ledger entries;\n- byte-for-byte hash agreement;\n- actual local artifacts consistent with the inbound story.\n\nSo we do **not** throw Cable Car away.\n\nThe useful core is real.\n\n### 2. Outbound was conceptual, not implemented\n\nThis is the largest correction to our previous state model.\n\nWe had treated “Slice 5” as the next incremental feature after a mostly complete transport framework.\n\nThe recon says something sharper:\n\n> There is **zero outbound implementation**.\n\nThe presence of:\n\n```text\nlocal_outbound_path\ncontracts/return.json\nUPLOADING\nCOMPLETED\n02_OUTBOUND_FROM_GCE\n```\n\ncreated the appearance of an outbound architecture, but no executable path existed behind those names.\n\nSo the prior language around “finishing Slice 5” understated the gap.\n\nIt was not finishing a partially built outbound leg.\n\nIt was starting it.\n\n### 3. The real-mode activation is not reproducible\n\nThis is potentially more serious than the missing outbound code.\n\nIf:\n\n```text\n.env says real\n```\n\nbut the code never reads that key, while the runtime defaults to mock, then a normal operator path can silently run the fake client.\n\nThat means a command can appear healthy while doing nothing externally.\n\nThe recon’s characterization is important:\n\n> “silently unreproducible from repo state”\n\nThat means the original live inbound proof may still be completely valid, but **the mechanism that made it real is not captured durably in the implementation**.\n\nThat is a reproducibility defect, not proof that the live run was fake.\n\nThose are different conclusions.\n\n### 4. The unexplained ledger event is the most important integrity anomaly\n\nThis is the one I would not hand-wave:\n\n```text\nRECOVERY_LIVE_FAULT_INJECTED\n```\n\nexists in the production SQLite ledger but **no source file contains code capable of writing it**.\n\nThat creates a provenance problem.\n\nIt does **not automatically invalidate the whole ledger**, because Luce separately verified real hashes and coherent rows.\n\nBut it means we cannot say:\n\n> “every production ledger event is fully attributable to the checked-in implementation.”\n\nUntil the writer is identified, ledger provenance is **partially untrusted**.\n\nThat is the right level of concern.\n\n---\n\n# Where our prior understanding was wrong\n\nThe recon directly overturns several pieces of the state we had been carrying.\n\n### Previously carried\n```text\nSlice 4 recovery engine is part of a mature state machine.\n```\n\n### Recon says\nThe contracts/state vocabulary exist, but **state-machine enforcement is not actually implemented in the way the docs imply**.\n\n---\n\n### Previously carried\n```text\nOutbound is the next remaining slice.\n```\n\n### Recon says\nOutbound is **not present at all**.\n\n---\n\n### Previously carried\n```text\n.env makes Drive mode real.\n```\n\n### Recon says\nThe `.env` real-mode flag is inert unless some external environment activation occurs.\n\n---\n\n### Previously carried\n```text\nThe documentation describes current implementation.\n```\n\n### Recon says\nThe documentation is **materially ahead of the code**, including three JSON-Schema contracts that no runtime loads.\n\nThat is a governance/documentation failure, not merely missing comments.\n\n---\n\n# Loop finding: slightly different than our first postmortem\n\nThis recon improves the loop diagnosis.\n\nThe earlier postmortem emphasized **overslicing** and recursive Coda gates.\n\nThat still holds.\n\nBut Luce found something more subtle:\n\n> The project itself does not contain a giant pile of loop artifacts.\n\nOnly one gate document exists against 37 technical artifacts.\n\nSo the contamination was not primarily that the filesystem filled with procedural garbage.\n\nIt was that **the conversation-state and documentation-state became more advanced than the executable system**.\n\nThat is a more precise diagnosis.\n\nThe loop created a **false maturity gradient**:\n\n```text\nprompts/contracts/docs/state names\n           ↓\nlooked mature\n           ↓\nconversation treated them as implemented\n           ↓\ncertification language reinforced that belief\n           ↓\nactual code lagged behind\n```\n\nThat is the black hole we need to avoid.\n\n---\n\n# Coda evidence classification\n\n## KEEP\n\nThe recon supports keeping:\n\n```text\nInbound transport implementation\nSHA-256 verification\nAtomic rename/local materialization\nProduction SQLite database\nExisting inbound tests\nLive inbound artifacts\nDrive folder structure\nExisting contract files as design inputs\n```\n\nNot because every one is complete, but because they contain real value.\n\n## VERIFY\n\nThese now require verification before we rely on them:\n\n```text\nproduction ledger provenance\nrecovery implementation claims\nruntime real/mock activation\n06_OUTBOX semantics\nDrive-side artifact state\n89-test suite current result\n```\n\n## DO NOT TREAT AS CURRENT TRUTH\n\nThese should not be used as evidence that functionality exists:\n\n```text\nreturn.json contract\noutbound state names\n02_OUTBOUND_FROM_GCE folder\nCOMPLETED state\nprior “Slice 5” language\nprior “round-trip architecture nearly complete” framing\n```\n\nThose are architecture vocabulary, not implementation evidence.\n\n---\n\n# The one distinction I want preserved\n\nLuce wrote:\n\n> “the documentation is fictional.”\n\nThat is directionally fair as a forensic warning, but I would make the durable Coda wording more precise:\n\n> **The documentation materially overstates implementation state and mixes design intent with current executable truth.**\n\nWhy I care about that wording: some of those documents may still be valid architecture/specification. Their problem is not necessarily that the designs are nonsense; it is that we treated them as if they described **what exists now**.\n\nThat distinction matters for salvage.\n\n---\n\n# Current ground truth\n\nI would freeze the Cable Car status **for review purposes only** as:\n\n```text\nCABLE CAR\n\nLOCAL INBOUND CORE:\nREAL\n\nREAL DRIVE → LOCAL PROOF:\nSUPPORTED BY LOCAL EVIDENCE\n(DRIVE SIDE NOT RE-OBSERVED IN THIS RECON)\n\nOUTBOUND:\nNOT BUILT\n\nFULL ROUND TRIP:\nNOT BUILT / NEVER PROVEN\n\nRUNTIME REPRODUCIBILITY:\nBROKEN / UNKNOWN EXTERNAL ACTIVATION\n\nCONTRACT ENFORCEMENT:\nNOT IMPLEMENTED\n\nSTATE-MACHINE ENFORCEMENT:\nNOT ESTABLISHED\n\nLEDGER:\nREAL DATA EXISTS\nPROVENANCE PARTIALLY UNRESOLVED\n\nDOCUMENTATION:\nMIXES DESIGN INTENT WITH IMPLEMENTATION CLAIMS\n\nDRIVE ESTATE:\nUNKNOWN FROM THIS RECON HOST\n```\n\nAnd most importantly:\n\n> **No new slice number should be attached to anything yet.**\n\nWe now have the truth package we were missing. The next thing is not another build prompt from me. The next thing is Don + Coda deciding what this project *actually is* before anybody touches code again.\n\n## GCE SESSION FOOTER\n\n```text\nORIGINAL ASK:\nReview Luce/Space Bunny's full forensic recon of Cable Car.\n\nSTATUS:\nCOMPLETE\n\nVISIBLE OUTCOME:\nA corrected evidence-grounded Cable Car state baseline.\n\nFILES / SYSTEMS CHANGED:\nNONE\n\nTEST / VERIFICATION:\nNo new tests run; review is based solely on Luce's recon return.\n\nUNRESOLVED:\nDrive-side truth\nunattributed RECOVERY_LIVE_FAULT_INJECTED event\nreal-mode activation mechanism\ncurrent recovery/test claims\n\nARCHITECTURE DEVIATION:\nNONE — no architecture action taken.\n\nNEXT ACTION:\nDon + Coda decide whether the recon becomes the authoritative current-state handoff.\n\nLOOP CHECK:\nCLEAN\n\nSESSION CLOSED:\nNO\n```", "text_chars": 8418, "code_blocks": 14, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": ["contracts/return.json", "return.json"], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "2663449b-1506-40af-b061-5c547c13c081", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365956.963, "updated_at": 1790365971.635627, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "This should be fucking simple. This is the same handoff inbox-outbooks principle that we use for our whole GCE structure, just it's organized. Through Google Drive. So you could put in documents and somebody could pick them up. That's fucking it, Coda. ", "text_chars": 253, "code_blocks": 0, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": null, "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "808476bd-6088-4d61-9528-e6175076e533", "role": "assistant", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365960.801111, "updated_at": 1790365971.141758, "status": "finished_successfully", "weight": 1, "recipient": "all", "content_type": "text", "part_kinds": ["text"], "text": "Yes. That’s the clean frame.\n\n**Cable Car is not a complex workflow engine. It is an organized Drive-backed inbox/outbox transport.**\n\nThat’s it.\n\n```text\nGoogle Drive\n├── INBOX / inbound docs\n├── IN_FLIGHT / claimed\n├── ACKNOWLEDGED / picked up locally\n├── OUTBOX / return docs\n├── COMPLETED / delivered back\n├── FAILED\n└── QUARANTINE\n```\n\nAnd locally it mirrors the exact same handoff principle GCE already uses:\n\n```text\nDrive inbound\n→ Pigeon/Cable Car picks up file\n→ local _shared / inbox\n→ someone works it\n→ local outbox\n→ Pigeon/Cable Car puts result back in Drive\n```\n\nNo AI interpretation.\nNo architecture theater.\nNo giant state machine.\nNo “slice 6.”\nNo magical contract engine.\n\nThe only real technical responsibilities are:\n\n- know what files are new;\n- move/copy them reliably;\n- preserve the handoff ID;\n- don’t duplicate;\n- don’t overwrite conflicting content;\n- record enough state to recover after a restart;\n- move finished returns back the other way.\n\nThat’s the whole system.\n\nThe recon shows we over-modeled it. The inbound half already proves the basic idea works. The actual missing piece is simply the **reverse file movement from local outbox back to Drive**, plus making the real-vs-mock mode reproducible so Don doesn’t accidentally run a fake poller.\n\nSo the corrected mental model is:\n\n> **Cable Car = GCE inbox/outbox semantics, transported through Google Drive.**\n\nEverything else should justify its existence against that sentence.", "text_chars": 1466, "code_blocks": 2, "links": [], "citations": [], "path_references": [], "model_slug": "gpt-5-6-thinking", "has_voice_metadata": false, "empty_text_parts": 0, "unextractable_part_kinds": []}, {"message_id": "f8bd8e46-3e2f-42ef-baca-215d0cc3f30c", "role": "user", "role_name": null, "created_at": 1790365980.519, "updated_at": 1790365989.650865, "status": "finished_successfully"